# PLAN v12: zsh array-subscript file detector (grep -Ef).
# zsh arrays are 1-based; the zsh frontend normalizes subscripts to the
# canonical 0-based A1 form at emit, which the core's bash parse cannot
# match byte-for-byte — the zsh gate waives byte-equality for files these
# patterns match (executed-stdout is the semantic anchor). Length forms
# (`${#x}`, `${#a[@]}`) deliberately do NOT match: no subscript, and they
# stay byte-compared. One alternative per line.
# zsh `:modifier` forms (`${x:l}`/`${x:u}`/`${x:t}`/`${x:h}`) — the core's
# bash parse reads `${x:l}` as a SLICE (bash semantics), while the
# frontend emits the zsh-correct unambiguous op (`^^`/`,,`/basename/
# dirname). Same waiver rationale: the core cannot parse the zsh form
# faithfully, so byte-equality is skipped and executed-stdout anchors.
# The pattern requires the modifier immediately before `}` — `${x:l:5}`
# (a real slice) and `${x:2}` (numeric offset) stay byte-compared.
\$[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\[
\$\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\[
(^|[[:space:]])[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\[
\$\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(:(l|u|t|h))\}
